{"authored" [{"question" "q-281586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $-4x+y+1=0$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "5eea7e91a9abc0d58092dce40dc68afaebe713a32d097ba5407f3506df1c80f1" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $-4x+y+1=0$ passes through the points $P(0,-1)$ and $Q(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 6 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(-1,-2)$ and ${Q}^{′}(6,6).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{6-(-2)}{6-(-1)}(x+1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{8}{7}(x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7y+14 & =8x+8 \\\\ 8x-7y & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n8x-7y=6\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "5eea7e91a9abc0d58092dce40dc68afaebe713a32d097ba5407f3506df1c80f1" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281586" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1c51e49536a811c52da04573453ecaf29d405a6bb4368af15d5a0ff0c364ff3f" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281586" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/state.json" "file_sha256" "5eea7e91a9abc0d58092dce40dc68afaebe713a32d097ba5407f3506df1c80f1" "basis" 869 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980539/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=3$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=21.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=3$ passes through the points $P(0,3)$ and $Q(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $P$ and $Q$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}9 & 10 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${P}^{′}(9,3)$ and ${Q}^{′}(10,1).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${P}^{′}$ and ${Q}^{′}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-3 & =\\frac{1-3}{10-9}(x-9) \\\\ y-3 & =-2(x-9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-2x+18 \\\\ 2x+y & =21\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281581" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "9974f5324429a17f7489734597819e89bf56de56adc0da00283f8fdca36de4cc" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281581" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-281581" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2x+y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/state.json" "file_sha256" "44393a51341cdbc33878d3fa6e77dfe3d2d410e602d7b37042a4ac554ba79cb3" "basis" 982 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13988920/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-77776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the line $y=4x-1$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012907/state.json" "file_sha256" "b42bfa467b538d9b8d7291413bd6a0bb059e06cb6a67a46b3c779b921a2e4ee1" "basis" 1163 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012907/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-77776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, notice that the line $y=4x-1$ passes through the points $Q(0,-1)$ and $R(1,3)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $Q$ and $R$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -10 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${Q}^{′}(3,-2)$ and ${R}^{′}(-10,6).$\n\nFinally, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${Q}^{′}$ and ${R}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-2) & =\\frac{6-(-2)}{-10-3}(x-3) \\\\ y+2 & =-\\frac{8}{13}(x-3) \\\\ 13y+26 & =-8x+24 \\\\ 8x+13y+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012907/state.json" "file_sha256" "b42bfa467b538d9b8d7291413bd6a0bb059e06cb6a67a46b3c779b921a2e4ee1" "basis" 1163 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012907/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-77776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012907/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "202a2e0f4a0652e6263fb253f02cfef9dfd32dc8ec431618ecea2cf72c1a7fc2" "basis" 1163 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012907/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-77776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14012907/state.json" "file_sha256" "b42bfa467b538d9b8d7291413bd6a0bb059e06cb6a67a46b3c779b921a2e4ee1" "basis" 1163 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-c